kernel.c: four bocl_error() calls pass the message as FNAME

kernel.c ~2192, ~2207, ~2222, ~2243 (parse of external-format options):

    bocl_error("Invalid encoding %s",
               string_cstring(symbol_name(encoding)));

bocl_error's signature is (fname, message_format, ...): these calls pass
the human message as FNAME and the ARGUMENT STRING as the format string.
Consequences: the report reads "In Invalid encoding %s: <name>" — and the
argument-derived string is interpreted as a printf FORMAT (a symbol name
containing %s/%n would read/write through garbage varargs).

Fix: bocl_error(__FUNCTION__, "Invalid encoding %s", ...) — same for the
other three.  Found while renaming error->bocl_error (tcc-lane ticket);
behavior-preserving rename kept as-is, so this pre-existing quirk is
tracked here instead.

RESOLUTION (2026-07-18) — v1.8.53
---------------------------------
All four calls fixed: bocl_error(__FUNCTION__, "Invalid ... %s",
string_static_cstr(prin1_to_string(obj))) — the message is now the
format string (no more format-string injection through symbol names
containing % directives; verified with a |%n%n%n| external-format
probe), and the printed-object cstring uses the static rotating buffer
instead of leaking a malloc on the longjmp error path.

Bonus, same theme: boerror.h defined __FUNCTION__ as the literal string
"some function" in strict ISO modes, so EVERY kernel error said
"In some function:".  C99/C11 guarantee __func__, so __FUNCTION__ now
aliases it and every ERROR/TYPE_ERROR names the real C function
(e.g. "In normalize_encoding: Invalid encoding :NO-SUCH-ENC").
44/44 suites green.
